      "result": "All 13 checklist items have been tested and recorded — **11 PASS, 2 FAIL**.\n\n**Summary of the two failures:**\n\n- **IX-12 (360° rotation)** — The panorama's `yaw` is never normalized modulo 360, so the view never wraps. At `yaw=0` two hotspots render; after rotating exactly one full turn to `yaw=360` (same zoom) *nothing* renders. Hotspots exist only in a single non-repeating band, and I swept ~18,000px of drag (to `yaw=3300` and `yaw=-1650`) without any reappearing. Notably, the dataset places hotspots across the full −160°…+160° range, confirming they were *meant* to wrap. The zoom half of this item was fine (clamped 0.5–2.0, hotspots correctly restored on zoom-out).\n\n- **CT-11 (image relevance)** — `HeritageCard`'s `onError` swaps in a hardcoded fallback URL that is another item's image (苏绣's `images[1]`). Since 春节's own first image is broken, its card ends up showing an unrelated item's image — which is itself also broken, leaving a broken image with no placeholder, unlike the detail page which handles failures properly.\n\n**Two methodology notes worth flagging:**\n\n1. Standard Playwright interactions did not work on the panorama and I had to fall back to `browser_evaluate` for two distinct reasons. Clicks on hotspots failed the \"element is stable\" check because hotspot positions are recalculated every frame, and `dragTo` silently did nothing because the rotation handlers (`onMouseDown/Move/Up`) live on the `panorama.hotspots` layer while my drag endpoint was a *non-descendant* zoom button, so `onMouseMove` never fired. Once I targeted that layer directly, dispatched mouse events drove rotation correctly and matched real click behavior (real clicks on the zoom buttons produced identical state changes).\n\n2. For IX-12 and CT-11 I read React fiber state and Vite-served source files rather than relying on the DOM alone. This was necessary to distinguish \"hotspots are off-screen\" from \"the view genuinely doesn't wrap,\" and to prove the 春节 image is a *fallback substitution* rather than simply a bad data entry. Both conclusions are independently corroborated by observable DOM behavior, so neither verdict depends solely on source inspection.\n\nOne judgment call to be transparent about: CT-11 asks whether images are topically relevant to each item, and DOM-only testing cannot verify that an Unsplash photo actually *depicts* embroidery or calligraphy. I verified the checkable part — each item has a distinct, dedicated image set with matching alt text — and failed the item on the one concrete, provable mismatch. If genuine visual subject-matter validation is required, that would need a different approach than this DOM-only mandate allows.",
